## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-tenant adapter design under strict hardware and privacy constraints; evaluates decision rationale for injection points, quantization, and evaluation rigor.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant adapter design and privacy guarantees
- Adapter types: LoRA, QLoRA, PEFT and their trade-offs
- Injection points: attention first, FFN fallback
- Quantization, step budgets, and gradient checkpointing
- Evaluation: latency, factual accuracy, privacy leakage tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-train step applying per-tenant adapters
function trainStep(batch, tenantId) {
  const baseOut = model.forward(batch.inputs);
  const adapOut = adapter[tenantId].apply(baseOut);
  const loss = lossFn(batch.targets, adapOut) + adapterReg(tenantId);
  optimizer.step(loss);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate tenant isolation in logs and metrics?
- What rollback strategy would you use if a tenant adapter regresses performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Understanding cross-language adapter strategies with strict latency and memory constraints.

## Key Concepts
- PEFT vs full fine-tuning in multi-language QA
- Adapter injection points (attention vs MLP)
- Language tagging vs per-language adapters
- Latency/memory budgeting and rollback plans

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo adapter injection sketch
function attachAdapters(model, keys) {
  for (const m of model.modules()) {
    if (m instanceof AttentionLayer || m instanceof MLP) {
      m.addAdapter({rank: 8, name: keys.next()});
    }
  }
  return model;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor drift between languages post-deployment?
- What failure modes require bypassing adapters and using base model instead?

